        -:    1:/* SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception */
        -:    2:/* hello_gpu: the smallest complete Omega program. It adds two arrays of one million floats on the GPU
        -:    3: * (c[i] = a[i] + b[i]) and checks every answer on the CPU.
        -:    4: *
        -:    5: * What makes it "Omega": the GPU program (the kernel) is written below as a short list of machine-level
        -:    6: * steps, handed to Omega's own code generator, and run through Omega's own launcher. Nothing here uses
        -:    7: * CUDA, nvcc, PTX or any NVIDIA user-space library.
        -:    8: *
        -:    9: * The five steps, in the order they appear in main():
        -:   10: *   1. Build the kernel      build_vecadd()   a list of IR instructions, then register allocation
        -:   11: *   2. Open the GPU          omega_gpu_session_open()
        -:   12: *   3. Upload kernel + data  omega_gpu_session_alloc_code(), omega_gpu_session_alloc()
        -:   13: *   4. Launch                omega_gpu_session_launch()   (eight launches of 131072 elements each)
        -:   14: *   5. Check                 every one of the 1048576 outputs against the CPU's own a + b
        -:   15: *
        -:   16: * Prints PASS or FAIL. Exit code: 0 PASS, 1 FAIL (wrong or missing results), 2 the GPU could not be used.
        -:   17: *
        -:   18: * `hello_gpu --sim` runs the same kernel program on the host warp simulator instead (tests/bw_warp_sim.h):
        -:   19: * no GPU is opened. A bad memory access there stops at the launch that made it. The simulator names the
        -:   20: * pc. This program names the block, thread, address and buffer from the launch it issued (developer bar
        -:   21: * D3 and D4, docs/PAIN_POINTS.md): the mirrored simulator does not store those fields. `--sim --bug=past-end`
        -:   22: * shows such a report: the last launch is given one element too many, a classic off-by-one.
        -:   23: *
        -:   24: * `--bug=short` is the negative control for the check itself, on the GPU or with --sim: the last launch is
        -:   25: * given one element too FEW, so c[1048575] is never written. That writes nothing outside a buffer, so it is
        -:   26: * safe on the chip; the check must report "1 never written", print FAIL and exit 1. `make hello-gpu-control`
        -:   27: * builds a separate binary, build/hello_gpu_control, with it and passes only when it goes red that way.
        -:   28: * MIGRATION-PENDING C (GpuStack PLAN, Language rule): it exists only when CUDAPP_MIGRATION_PENDING_HELLO_CONTROL
        -:   29: * is defined to 1, which is OFF by default (build/hello_gpu never has it). Its .om successor is the native .om
        -:   30: * hello of aien-dev/CUDApp#79 (OM-GPU-RETIRE), which replaces this whole C program.
        -:   31: *
        -:   32: * Both paths print the work the job takes (bytes moved, floating-point operations) and the least time
        -:   33: * those bytes need at this GB10's measured read ceiling (D12). The mirrored simulator no longer counts
        -:   34: * those bytes, so --sim accounts the vecadd this program issued: two f32 loads, one f32 store and one
        -:   35: * FADD per element. Both paths also print the time the kernel took to build, the
        -:   36: * hash of its machine code (D10, no compiling at run time), and the hash of the output (D7: the same
        -:   37: * inputs give the same bits on every run).
        -:   38: *
        -:   39: * Hardware: NVIDIA GB10 (DGX Spark, sm_121), Linux, the open NVIDIA kernel driver loaded.
        -:   40: * Built by `make hello-gpu` (see README, "Run your first kernel").
        -:   41: */
